Given this schema and data, predict the result of the final query and explain the mechanism:

```sql
CREATE TABLE teams (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT);
CREATE TABLE players (id INT PRIMARY KEY, team_id INT REFERENCES teams(id) ON DELETE CASCADE);
INSERT INTO teams VALUES (1, 'Warriors'), (2, 'Lakers');
INSERT INTO players VALUES (10, 1), (11, 1), (12, 2);
DELETE FROM teams WHERE id = 1;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM players;
```

content.reference_answer:
Defects: 1. Inefficient querying: Finding articles with tag 'sql' requires `LIKE '%sql%'` full-table scans that cannot use B-tree indexes and risk false positives (e.g. matching 'nosql'). 2. Integrity: The database cannot enforce foreign keys on individual tags.
Normalized 1NF Schema:
```sql
CREATE TABLE articles (id INT PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE tags (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE article_tags (
    article_id INT REFERENCES articles(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    tag_id INT REFERENCES tags(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    PRIMARY KEY (article_id, tag_id)
);
```
